{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Educational Technology Evaluation","title":"Peer Review of Evaluation Briefs","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting peer review of evaluation briefs to equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"07","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","peer_review","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/05/06"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_5_7_peer_review_of_evaluation_briefs_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_5_7_peer_review_of_evaluation_briefs_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_5_7_peer_review_of_evaluation_briefs_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_5_7_peer_review_of_evaluation_briefs_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Peer Review of Evaluation Briefs\n\n![Peer Review of Evaluation Briefs textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/05-07-peer-review-of-evaluation-briefs.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nReviewing a colleague's evaluation brief is a different skill from writing your own, and most teachers default to the wrong one. The instinct is to react: comment on the font, say which tool you would have picked, note that the writing is clean. None of that tests whether the brief's conclusion is actually earned by the brief's own evidence. Useful peer review does one thing above all else: it checks the link between what the author claimed and what the author showed, and it leaves preference and polish at the door. This section trains review as evidence-to-conclusion auditing rather than reaction.\n\nThis matters because a brief that survives weak review carries a hidden flaw into a real adoption decision. If a reviewer praises a polished brief whose conclusion outruns its data, the polish becomes a liability: it makes an unsupported recommendation look trustworthy. The reviewer's job is to be the reader who refuses to be reassured by surface and insists the reasoning hold.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Distinguish a review that tests the evidence-to-conclusion link from one that reacts to style or personal preference.\n- Read a brief against its own stated question and locate where the conclusion outruns the evidence.\n- Write comments that name the specific gap and propose the evidence that would close it.\n- Remove preference and polish comments that do not test the author's reasoning.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nYou are assigned to peer review a colleague's evaluation brief for a discussion-board tool. Your first reaction is to write \"looks polished, I would have picked a different tool, maybe change the heading font.\" Then you notice the brief's stated question is whether the tool supports equitable participation for quiet students, its conclusion is \"adopt,\" and its evidence section reports only total posting volume, with nothing about who is posting.\n\nThe decision worth recording is not your impression of the brief. It is whether you can see that \"supports equitable participation\" is not demonstrated by a rise in total posts, since the same few students could be driving the increase, and whether your comments test that gap rather than your own taste.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEvery evaluation brief makes an implicit syllogism: here is my question, here is my evidence, therefore here is my conclusion. Peer review audits the joints of that syllogism, especially the **evidence-to-conclusion link**, the place where a conclusion can quietly claim more than the evidence shows.\n\nThe most common failure is a **construct mismatch**: the conclusion is about one thing (equitable participation) and the evidence measures another (total volume). Total posting volume can rise while participation becomes less equitable, because a few prolific students can produce the entire increase. The evidence is real and the conclusion is plausible, but the evidence does not measure the thing the conclusion claims. A reviewer's central move is to ask, for each conclusion, does the evidence offered actually measure this claim?\n\nThree review behaviors separate testing from reacting:\n\n- **Test the link, not the topic.** You may have chosen a different tool; that is irrelevant unless the author's evidence fails to support the author's conclusion.\n- **Name the gap precisely.** \"Weak evidence\" is not actionable; \"total posts cannot show equitable participation, you need a per-student distribution\" is.\n- **Propose the missing evidence.** A good review tells the author what would close the gap, turning criticism into a revision path.\n\nThree course anchors keep review substantive. **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** helps you check whether the brief's evidence even measures a learning construct or just an activity count. **Critical edtech studies** (Selwyn, 2016; Perrotta & Selwyn, 2019) sharpens the equity reads, where conclusions most often outrun aggregate numbers. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** prompts you to question metrics the tool itself reports, since a vendor dashboard may count what is easy rather than what matters.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A brief's stated question is whether a discussion-board tool supports equitable participation for quiet students. Its conclusion is 'adopt.' Its only evidence is that total posting volume rose. Where is the flaw?\" explanation=\"This is a construct mismatch. The conclusion claims equitable participation, but total volume can rise while a few prolific students produce the entire increase. The evidence measures activity, not the distribution of who participates, so the conclusion outruns the evidence. The fix is a per-student distribution of posts.\" options='[{\"text\":\"There is no flaw; more posts means more participation.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The evidence (total volume) does not measure the construct (equitable participation); a few students could drive the rise.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The flaw is that the writing is too polished.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nStart with a thin claim that many students write on a first pass:\n\n> I made the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies** (Selwyn, 2016), and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nThe stronger version treats the matrix as evidence: it names what changed and leaves a reader able to audit the decision.\n\n### Worked Example: Reviewing the Reasoning, Not the Polish\n\nScenario: you are assigned to peer review a colleague's evaluation brief for a discussion-board tool.\n\n1. **Weak review draft (before).** Your first comments read: \"Looks polished. I would have picked a different tool. Maybe change the heading font.\" None of these test whether the brief's conclusion holds.\n2. **Read the brief against its own question.** The brief's question is \"Does the tool support equitable participation for quiet students?\" The conclusion is \"Adopt.\" But the evidence section reports only overall posting volume, with nothing about who is posting.\n3. **Located the gap.** The conclusion claims equity of participation; the evidence measures total volume, which can rise while the same few students dominate. The conclusion outruns the evidence.\n4. **Annotated judgment call (after).** Your revised review reads: \"Strong question and clear writing. The gap is in the evidence-to-conclusion link: 'supports equitable participation' is not shown by total post counts. Suggest adding a distribution of posts per student before keeping the adopt conclusion. I removed my font and tool-preference comments because they do not test your reasoning.\"\n\nThe review is defensible because every comment ties back to whether the author's own evidence supports the author's own conclusion.\n\nNow do the review yourself. The scenario hands you the discussion-board brief and tests whether you audit the reasoning or react to the surface.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Reviewing the discussion-board brief: reasoning, not polish\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"You are assigned to peer review a colleague\\\\u2019s evaluation brief for a discussion-board tool. What is your opening move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Comment on the polish, say you would have picked a different tool, suggest a heading-font change\",\"next\":\"react\",\"feedback\":\"None of these test whether the conclusion holds.\"},{\"label\":\"Read the brief against its own stated question first\",\"next\":\"question\",\"feedback\":\"You start from the author\\\\u2019s question, not your taste.\"}]},\"react\":{\"prompt\":\"You send three comments: looks polished, different tool preferred, change the font. The author accepts all three and ships. What is still wrong with the brief?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The unsupported conclusion is untouched; your comments tested taste, not reasoning\",\"next\":\"question\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Now review the actual argument.\"}]},\"question\":{\"prompt\":\"The brief\\\\u2019s question is \\\"Does the tool support equitable participation for quiet students?\\\" Its conclusion is \\\"adopt.\\\" The evidence section reports only total posting volume. What is the gap?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No gap; rising posts shows participation\",\"next\":\"nogap\",\"feedback\":\"Total volume can rise while a few students dominate; look again.\"},{\"label\":\"Construct mismatch: the conclusion claims equity, the evidence measures total volume\",\"next\":\"locate\",\"feedback\":\"You found where the conclusion outruns the evidence.\"}]},\"nogap\":{\"prompt\":\"A class where three students triple their posts and everyone else stays silent also shows rising total volume. Does that count as equitable participation?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; the total cannot tell those two classes apart\",\"next\":\"locate\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. The metric is blind to the distribution.\"}]},\"locate\":{\"prompt\":\"You have named the gap. A good review does one more thing. What?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Just flag \\\"weak evidence\\\" and move on\",\"next\":\"weak\",\"feedback\":\"\\\"Weak evidence\\\" is not actionable; the author has no path to revise.\"},{\"label\":\"Propose the specific missing evidence: a per-student distribution of posts\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"You turn criticism into a revision path.\"}]},\"weak\":{\"prompt\":\"The author asks what would fix it. What do you name?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A per-student distribution of posts, to show whether participation is equitable\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Now the comment is actionable.\"}]},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: praise the clear question and writing, name the evidence-to-conclusion gap (total volume cannot show equitable participation), propose the per-student distribution, and remove the font and tool-preference comments because they do not test the reasoning.\",\"feedback\":\"This is review as evidence-to-conclusion auditing, the only thing peer review is for.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nUse the brief's own question first, the evidence-to-conclusion link second, and your stylistic reactions last (usually not at all). A review comment is useful only when it tests whether the author's evidence supports the author's conclusion.\n\nFor each conclusion in a brief, run three checks:\n\n1. **Question check:** What did the brief set out to determine, and does the conclusion answer that question?\n2. **Link check:** Does the evidence offered actually measure the construct the conclusion claims, or a proxy that can move independently of it?\n3. **Closure check:** If there is a gap, what specific evidence would close it, and can you name it for the author?\n\nA comment that survives these checks belongs in the review; a comment about font, layout, or your own tool preference does not, unless it changes whether the reasoning holds.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is mistaking polish for soundness; clean writing can carry an unearned conclusion, and praising the polish reinforces the flaw. The second is reviewing the tool instead of the brief, substituting your choice for an audit of the author's argument. The third is flagging \"weak evidence\" without naming the gap or the fix, which leaves the author no path to revise.\n\nA defensible review ties every comment to the author's own question and evidence, and proposes the specific data that would close any gap it identifies.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake the discussion-board brief and review it twice. The first pass is the reflex pass: polished, different tool preferred, font note. Read those three comments back and ask what each one tests about the brief's reasoning. None of them do; they describe your taste and your hypothetical choice, and the author could accept all three and still ship an unsupported conclusion.\n\nThe second pass starts from the brief's own question: equitable participation for quiet students. Now read the evidence section against that question. It reports total posting volume, which is a count of activity, not a measure of who participates. The conclusion \"adopt, supports equitable participation\" rests on evidence that cannot distinguish a class where everyone posts more from a class where three students post much more.\n\nWrite the review that names this exactly: praise the clear question and writing, then state the link failure and the fix, \"a per-student distribution of posts would show whether participation is equitable; the current total cannot.\" Remove the font and preference comments, and say you removed them. The review is now an audit of the author's reasoning, which is the only thing peer review is for.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a brief, keep a three-column note: the author's question, the evidence offered, and the conclusion drawn. The review writes itself from the gaps between the columns, especially where the conclusion's construct does not match the evidence's measure. If a comment of yours does not point to one of those gaps, it is probably reaction, not review.\n\nA strong reviewer of an evaluation brief can answer four questions:\n\n1. What question did the brief set out to answer?\n2. Does the evidence offered measure the construct the conclusion claims?\n3. Where exactly does the conclusion outrun the evidence?\n4. What specific evidence would close the gap, and did I tell the author?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nFor this section, the review is strong when every comment ties to the brief's own question and evidence, names each evidence-to-conclusion gap precisely, and proposes the data that would close it, so a mentor can see the review tested reasoning rather than taste. Treat a comment about polish or personal tool preference as noise to be cut unless it changes whether the conclusion holds.\n\nA useful closing line would sound like this: \"I changed my review to focus on the link between the brief's evidence (total post volume) and its conclusion (equitable participation), and I proposed a per-student distribution as the missing measure. I removed my font and tool-preference comments because they did not test the author's reasoning.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove from reviewing a peer's brief to reviewing your own before submission, reading it as a hostile reviewer would. The same three columns apply: your question, your evidence, your conclusion. The discipline is harder on your own work because you know what you meant, so you must check whether the page actually shows it. A self-review that catches your own construct mismatch (you concluded \"improves learning\" but measured time-on-task) is worth more than any external comment. Use the shift to confirm that evidence-to-conclusion auditing is a stance you can take toward any brief, including the one you wrote.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/05-07-peer-review-of-evaluation-briefs-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"05.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes for CAT 531 05.07. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies (Selwyn, 2016). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain the difference between reviewing the evidence-to-conclusion link and reacting to a brief, and explain why praising a polished brief whose conclusion outruns its data is worse than saying nothing.\" answer=\"Reviewing the link means testing whether the author's own evidence actually measures the construct the conclusion claims, and naming the gap plus the data that would close it. Reacting means commenting on font, layout, or which tool you personally would have chosen, none of which touch whether the reasoning holds. Praising a polished but unsupported brief is worse than silence because the polish already makes an unearned conclusion look trustworthy; endorsing it adds the reviewer's credibility to a flaw and lets a construct mismatch travel into a real adoption decision unchallenged.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation\",\"peer_review\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_peer_review_of_evaluation_briefs\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm your review reads the brief against its own question, names where the conclusion outruns the evidence, proposes the specific data that would close the gap, and cuts comments about polish or personal preference that do not test the reasoning.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning et al. (2024), Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Selwyn, N. (2016), Is Technology Good for Education? Polity (critical edtech studies).\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2019), Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education ([AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017))\n- Khalil et al. (2023), Learning analytics in support of inclusiveness and disabled students: A systematic review. https://doi.org/10.1007/s12528-023-09363-4\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_7_peer_review_of_evaluation_briefs_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"peer_review","stem":"You are peer reviewing a colleague's evaluation brief. Which comment is the most useful review of the brief?","options":["'Nice work, looks great.'","'Your recommendation says adopt, but the privacy criterion is left blank; the conclusion outruns the evidence in the brief.'","'I would have chosen a different font.'","'I disagree because I prefer the other tool.'"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A strong peer review checks whether the brief's conclusion is supported by its own evidence, and points to the exact gap. Praise, style preferences, and personal taste do not improve the reasoning.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_7_review_is_praise","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","peer_review","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_7_peer_review_of_evaluation_briefs_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"peer_review","stem":"A reviewer writes 'I would have evaluated a different tool.' Why is this a weak review of an evaluation brief?","options":["Reviewers should never mention other tools.","It substitutes the reviewer's preference for an assessment of whether the author's reasoning is sound; review evaluates the brief, not the brief the reviewer would have written.","It is too short to count.","It is too harsh for a colleague."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Peer review evaluates the author's reasoning against their own question and evidence. Rewriting the brief in the reviewer's image is not review; it ignores the author's decision context.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_7_review_imposes_reviewer_choice","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","peer_review"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_7_peer_review_of_evaluation_briefs_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to peer review a colleague's brief. It returns polished feedback. What is the defensible way to use it?","options":["Forward the AI feedback to your colleague as your review.","Read the brief yourself, use the AI points as prompts, and only pass on critiques you have verified against the brief's actual evidence.","Trust the AI review because it is thorough.","Skip reading the brief since the AI already reviewed it."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An AI may flag generic issues that do not apply or miss context-specific gaps. The reviewer stays responsible for an accurate read, using the AI as a prompt rather than a substitute.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_7_ai_review_replaces_reviewer","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","peer_review"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_7_peer_review_of_evaluation_briefs_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A peer review flags that your brief's conclusion is not supported by its evidence. You are confident the conclusion is right. What best honors the review?","options":["Reject the comment because you are confident.","Either add the missing evidence that justifies the conclusion or revise the conclusion to match what the evidence shows.","Leave the brief unchanged and note that a reviewer disagreed.","Soften the wording so the gap is less visible."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A reviewer's job is to surface gaps; the author's job is to close them with evidence or to revise the claim. Confidence is not evidence, and hiding the gap defeats the purpose of review.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_7_confidence_answers_review","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision","peer_review"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"915278d88db18615e1f669d41d1810afbe714aa16323578c9ab83c73f2b11c56","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}